== 20. You Are the Lord’s Secret Ones, His Hidden Ones

In the last place, consider that you#super[+] alone are the Lord’s secret ones, His hidden ones; and therefore, if you do not apply yourselves to private prayer and to your secret retreats, so that you may enjoy God in a corner, no one will. It is only God’s hidden ones, His secret ones, who have the inclination, the principle, and the preparation to wait on God in secret. “… you#super[+] will be My treasured possession out of all the nations” (Exod 19:5). The Hebrew word #emph[segullah] signifies God’s special jewels, God’s own ones, or God’s secret ones, whom He keeps in store for Himself and for His own special service and use. Princes lock up their most precious and costly jewels in secret with their own hands; and so does God with His. “For the #smallcaps[Lord] has chosen Jacob as His own, Israel as His treasured possession” (Ps 135:4), or as His secret gem. “With cunning they scheme against Your people and conspire against Your hidden ones” (Ps 83:3), or Your secret ones. They are so called partly because God hides them in the secret of His tabernacle (Ps 31:20), and partly because God sets as high a value on them as men do on their hidden treasure, their secret treasure. Indeed, He sets more store by them than by all the rest of the world. And so the world will know when God arises to avenge the wrongs and injuries that have been done to His secret ones.

Nor are there any on earth who know so much of the secrets of His love, of the secrets of His counsels, of the secrets of His purposes, of the secrets of His heart, as His secret ones do. Nor are there any in all the world who are under those secret influences, those secret aids, those secret inflowings, those secret anointings of the Spirit, as His secret ones are. And therefore, no wonder if God calls them again and again and again His “secret ones.” Now, what can be more fitting or more desirable than to see their natures and their practices answer to their names? They are the Lord’s secret ones, His hidden ones; therefore, how much it concerns them to be much with God in secret, and to hide themselves with God in a corner! Shall Nabal’s nature and practice answer to his name? “My lord should pay no attention to this scoundrel Nabal, for he lives up to his name: Nabal is his name, and folly accompanies him” (1 Sam 25:25). Nabal signifies a fool, a dolt, a churl; it denotes one who is void of wisdom and goodness. It signifies one whose mind, reason, judgment, and understanding are withered and decayed. Now, if you#super[+] look into the story, you will find that as face answers to face, so Nabal’s nature and practice echoed and answered to his name. Why then should not our natures and practices answer to our names also? We are called the Lord’s secret ones, His hidden ones; and how much, therefore, it concerns us to be much with God in secret!

Why should there be any jarring or discord between our names and our practices? It is worth noting that the practice and conduct of other saints have matched their names. Isaac signifies laughter, and Isaac was a gracious son, a dutiful son, a son who kept clear of those abominations with which many of the patriarchs had defiled themselves, a son who proved a cause of laughter to his father and mother all their days. So Josiah signifies “the fire of the Lord,” and his practice answered to his name. Witness his pulling down of Jeroboam’s altar, and his burning of the vessels that were made for Baal, and his putting down the idolatrous priests whom the kings of Judah had set up, and his burning the Asherah pole at the brook Kidron, and his stamping it to powder, and his breaking down the houses of the male shrine prostitutes, and his defiling of the high places where the priests had burned incense, and his breaking the images in pieces, and cutting down the Asherah poles, and filling their places with the bones of men, etc. (1 Kgs 13:2; 2 Kgs 23:4–21). So Joshua signifies “a savior,” and his practice matched his name. Though he could not save his people from their sins, yet he often saved them from their sufferings. Great and many were the deliverances, the salvations, that were brought about through Joshua as God’s instrument, as all who have read the book of Joshua know. So John signifies “gracious,” and his practice matched his name. He was so gracious in his teaching and in his walk that he gained favor in the very eyes of his enemies.

By all these instances, and by many more that might be given, you#super[+] see that other saints’ practices have answered to their names. Therefore let every one of us see to it that our practices also answer to our names: that as we are called the Lord’s secret ones, so we may be much with God in secret; that so there may be a blessed harmony between our names and our practices; and that we may never regret, some day, that we have been called God’s secret ones, His “hidden ones” (Ps 83:3), and yet never made it a matter of conscience to maintain secret communion with God in our closets.

Thus you#super[+] see that there are no fewer than twenty arguments to persuade you to closet prayer, and to maintain private communion with God in a corner.
